"# aos-directive: resolve directives\n\n```sh\necho '{\"p\": {\"$env\": \"HOME\", \"$path\": \"/x\"}, \"r\": {\"$ref\": \"other.json#/x\"}}' | aos-directive\n# {\"p\": \"/home/me/x\", \"r\": {\"y\": \"hi\"}}\n```\n\nIt reads one JSON document from stdin (empty = `{}`), resolves every directive in it (`$ref`, `$env`, `$path`, ...; relative paths from the current directory) and prints the result as one line. An unset variable or an unreadable `$ref` = `DirectiveBad`; stdin that is not JSON = `InputBad`. Other aos programs (aos-exec, the clock register, tool registries) resolve the same directives themselves; use this to see what a config will turn into.\n"
